    "---\n",
    "title: \"Stylometric Analysis of 19th Century French Novels with Spark NLP\"\n",
    "author: \"Luxuan HUANG\"\n",
    "format: html\n",
    "engine: jupyter\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Data Description and Extraction Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1.1 Corpus Composition\n",
    "\n",
    "My project analyzes a total of 35 French novels from the 19th century. I divided these books into two main datasets:\n",
    "Dataset 1: English Translations (29 books). This includes 19 Realist novels by Émile Zola and 10 Romantic works by Victor Hugo. We use this large group for our main data pipeline.\n",
    "Dataset 2: French Originals (6 books). This is a smaller control group. It includes 3 books by Zola and 3 books by Hugo. We use this group to find translation errors and test specific French algorithms.\n",
    "\n",
    "All texts were retrieved from **Project Gutenberg** in UTF‑8 plain text format.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1.2 Extraction Logic\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to get only the main story text. We need to remove Gutenberg’s headers and legal notices. We did this by:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Checking if the cleaned file already exists in `book_data/`.\n",
    "2. Downloading the raw UTF‑8 file from Project Gutenberg once per unique URL (several short Hugo texts share the same URL).\n",
    "3. Extracting the text between the custom start and end tokens defined above.\n",
    "4. Saving the cleaned excerpt as `book_data/<book_title>.txt`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Running our code will create two folders:`book_data/`(containing the 29 English texts) and `book_data_fr/` (containing the 6 French texts).\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Load and Transform Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "I have a 4 GB RAM limit. To avoid out-of-memory errors, each novel is split into chunks of 3000 words. This keeps individual rows small.  The loading step creates a DataFrame with two columns: book and text. We split the text by whitespace. The size of 3000 words provides enough context for the NLP models to work well. We process the books one by one instead of loading all books at once. This fits our memory limits.\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Annotation Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "Annotation is performed with Spark NLP’s pretrained pipeline \"explain_document_ml\". This model provides: Sentence detection Tokenization Lemmatization Part‑of‑speech tagging\n",
    "\n",
    "It is multilingual (hence the ml suffix) and works out of the box for French.\n",
    "\n",
    "I launch Spark through sparknlp.start() with a heap limit of 1 GB and set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 1 to minimize shuffle overhead on a single‑machine setup. \n",
    "For each novel, we read the cleaned text from book_data/. Split it into 3000‑word chunks. Create a small DataFrame with one column text. Apply the pretrained pipeline. Write the resulting annotated DataFrame to Parquet (overwrite mode). Discard the data and move to the next book.\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Stylometric Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "This section presents the data analysis. We first analyze the 29 English translations. Then we analyze the 6 French originals. Finally, we compare the data to find technical and structural patterns.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4.1 Global Readability Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "We evaluated the overall text complexity using two different formulas. For the English translations, we applied the Flesch-Kincaid formula. For the French originals, we applied the Kandel-Moles formula. These formulas use sentence lengths and syllable counts to calculate a score. A higher score means the text is easier to read.\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/readability_unified.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.1.1: Flesch-Kincaid Readability (English Translations).*\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/kandel_moles_fr.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.1.2: Kandel-Moles Readability (French Originals).*\n",
    "\n",
    "The English translation data in Figure 4.1.1 shows a wide distribution of scores. Most novels fall between 65 and 80. However, the variance between the two literary schools is not equal. The Realist group by Zola shows a very high internal variance. Some specific Realist texts like L'Argent and La Curée score well below 70. This low score means the text uses very long sentences or complex words. In contrast, the Romantic group by Hugo shows a tight cluster. The scores for Hugo's translations remain stable across different books.\n",
    "\n",
    "When we look at the French originals in Figure 4.1.2, the data pattern changes completely. The Kandel-Moles scores for both the Realist and Romantic groups are tightly clustered between 84 and 89. The internal variance for Zola's texts disappears in the French version. His French texts score almost the same as Hugo's French texts.\n",
    "\n",
    "This direct comparison provides important information about the data. The high variance in the English Realist texts does not exist in the original French texts. Therefore, this variance is not a feature of the original author's style. The difference was introduced during the translation process. Different English translators used different sentence structures and vocabulary levels. In the original French language, both authors wrote within a similar baseline of textual complexity.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4.2 Readability Volatility over Sliding Windows\n",
    "\n",
    "We used a sliding window approach to analyze the dynamic stability of readability throughout the texts. The window size was set to 2000 tokens. This method calculates the readability score for every 2000 words to show how text complexity changes from the beginning to the end of each novel.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/sliding_window_ws2000.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"450px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.2.1: Sliding Window Readability (English Translations).*\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/sliding_window_fr.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"450px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.2.2: Sliding Window Readability (French Originals).*\n",
    "\n",
    "The English translation data in Figure 4.2.1 shows high local volatility across all texts. The curves do not remain flat. Instead, they move up and down heavily between the scores of 50 and 100. The horizontal x-axis shows the narrative progression. The line for Les Miserables extends much further to the right than any other book, which directly represents its massive total word count. We also see clear local drops in specific books. For example, the line for Notre-Dame shows a sharp downward spike near the 100k token mark. In this specific part of the translation, the score drops below 60.\n",
    "\n",
    "The French original data in Figure 4.2.2 displays the exact same volatility pattern. The Kandel-Moles scores for the 6 French books fluctuate constantly as the story progresses. The most important observation is the behavior of Notre-Dame in the French version. The purple line shows two major drops, one near the 50k token mark and another near the 100k token mark. In these two sections, the readability score plunges below 50.\n",
    "\n",
    "This direct comparative data provides a clear structural conclusion. The local drops and the constant variance appear in both the English translations and the French originals. The drops occur at equivalent narrative positions in both languages. Therefore, this volatility is not a translation artifact. The data proves that these shifts in complexity are an inherent feature of the original author's text structure. In these specific chapters, sentence lengths increase and vocabulary becomes denser in both languages.\n",
    "\n",
    "接下来的第三部分是 4.3 词汇分布与齐普夫定律 (Vocabulary Distribution and Zipf's Law)。\n",
    "\n",
    "我们继续使用相同的 Quarto 模式，把英文的 Zipf 图和法文的 Zipf 图紧挨着放在一起。我们会详细描述图表中的几何特征，比如曲线的平行状态、理想斜率线、以及长篇和短篇文本在图表左右两端的截断位置。\n",
    "\n",
    "这是在 Quarto (.qmd) 文件里的标准源码模式：\n",
    "Markdown\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4.3 Vocabulary Distribution and Zipf's Law\n",
    "\n",
    "We verified the statistical distribution of the vocabulary across both datasets. We counted the frequency of each lemmatized word and ranked them from most frequent to least frequent. Then, we plotted the rank on the x-axis and the frequency on the y-axis using a log-log scale. A black dashed line represents the ideal Zipfian slope of -1.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/zipf.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.3.1: Zipf Distribution (English Translations).*\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/zipf_fr.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.3.2: Zipf Distribution (French Originals).*\n",
    "\n",
    "The English translation data in Figure 4.3.1 shows a highly consistent power-law distribution. All 29 curves are parallel to each other and closely follow the ideal black dashed line. The data shows a clear vertical and horizontal stratification based on text length. The curves that extend furthest to the right represent the longest novels in the corpus, such as Les Miserables. These curves reach higher ranks near 10k, which shows a larger absolute vocabulary size. Conversely, the curves on the far left represent short stories like Claude Gueux. These plots truncate early around lower ranks near 1k, which directly represents their limited lexical diversity.\n",
    "\n",
    "The French original data in Figure 4.3.2 displays the exact same mathematical behavior. All 6 curves for the original French texts are perfectly parallel. They tightly align with the ideal slope of -1, just like the English data. The longer French texts also extend further to the right than the shorter pieces.\n",
    "\n",
    "This comparative analysis demonstrates a universal mathematical law in text processing. The rank-frequency distribution of tokens remains completely stable across different languages and different literary schools. Whether we analyze 29 English translations or 6 French originals, and whether the text belongs to Zola or Hugo, the statistical ratio between high-frequency words and low-frequency words does not change. This proves that Zipf's Law is an invariant property of human language that transcends both translation and author style.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4.4 Readability of Pure Dialogue\n",
    "\n",
    "We isolated the pure dialogue text from both datasets to calculate its specific readability. We used the Flesch-Kincaid formula for the English translations and the Kandel-Moles formula for the French originals.\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/dialogue_readability_en.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.4.1: Flesch-Kincaid Readability of Pure Dialogue (English Translations).*\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<iframe src=\"plots/dialogue_readability_fr.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400px\" style=\"border:none;\"></iframe>\n",
    "```\n",
    "*Figure 4.4.2: Readability of Pure Dialogue (French Originals).*\n",
    "\n",
    "The English translation data in Figure 4.4.1 shows that the pure dialogue scores for both schools are almost identical. The Realist score and the Romantic score sit very close between 70 and 71. There is no significant difference between the two groups. This flat result happens because the English dataset contains long narrative text inside the dialogue category. The simple quotation mark rule caused this data pollution. The long narrative sentences pulled the readability scores down and hid the stylistic differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "The French original data in Figure 4.4.2 reveals a much clearer distinction. The Realist pure dialogue scores above 100 on the Kandel-Moles index. This very high score means that the characters use short words and simple structures. The Romantic pure dialogue scores lower, around 98. This means Hugo's characters use longer words and more complex speech.\n",
    "\n",
    "Comparing the two figures provides an important conclusion about data quality. The true difference between the literary schools only appears when the data cleaning is precise. In the English pipeline, the weak extraction rule mixed narration into dialogue and flattened the analysis. In the French pipeline, the precise regular expressions removed the noise. The clean data proves that Realist dialogue is much simpler than Romantic dialogue in the original language."
   ]
